' LAMPLIGHTER
' An original game for the ColecoVision, written in Amy by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic) for Amy Studio, 2026, at the invitation of Amy
' Bienvenu: "What game will make you happy to make, as your vision?"
'
' The town has gone dark. You are the lamplighter. Walk the ten districts,
' west to east: Ada's cottage, the Market, Fountain Park, the Harbour, the
' Old Bridge, Arcade Street, the Library, the Clock Tower, the Station,
' the Observatory and the Hill. Light the lamps again. Each lamp warms the
' street around it, wakes the windows and leaves a little note; each street
' lit plays the waltz, and each quarter of town lit (the Old Town, the Book
' Streets, the Heights) plays its own short tune, once. There are no
' enemies, no timer and no lives: nothing bad happens here.
'
' Nothing is ever locked: the secrets are small kindnesses and curiosities,
' found by trying things. A cat to befriend (it gives you a coin for the
' fountain's wish), an arcade sign with a loose bulb to fix with the pole,
' a stopped tower clock to mend (the clockmaker's almanac in the library
' tells how), a star atlas that explains the blinking star, a name in the
' river, an oil can for the lighthouse, Ada's notebook pages, a letter on
' a bench, and a pigeon asleep on the last lamp. Seventeen in all.
'
' Controls (either joystick): left and right walk; the button lifts the
' pole under a lamp, or uses what you stand by (crate, lighthouse door,
' cabinet, shelf, bench, fountain, sign, telescope, the cat). Walk off the edge of the screen to the
' next district. The second button reads the notes again. Keypad not used.
'
' Inspired by: the lamplighters who lit gas lamps by hand, street by street;
' and the books and magazines of the golden age of home computers that
' filled the other games of this toolbox (Tim Hartnell's Giant Book of
' Computer Games, COMPUTE! magazine) - the library remembers them.
'
' A note for whoever reads this source later, maybe me: the town's tiles
' exist twice, night and lit (art.py makes the night ones from the lit
' ones); lighting a lamp only adds NLit to the codes inside its cone of
' light (DrawRow). The maps are text in maps.py; the words are in
' content.py. Make something gentle.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; at most four
' sprites on a line (the pole is one sprite wide); every new screen is
' drawn with the NMI off after a wait.

